A||||2||ThatRickGuy||Found a good mechanic south of DC, at a cost.||||1||
Z||000000||ThatRickGuy||02-07-2001||03:56 PM||||Hey, just thought I'd stick a msg out here that I found a guy w/ fiero experience. It's at the 'Quantico Service Station' just off I-95 (exit 150) about 1/2 an hour south of DC. I already liked the shop, very straight forward and good mechanics. The fiero guy there called me when I dropped my fiero off, unfortunatly with some bad news  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> my suspention rebuild just keeps getting more expencive  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>W/O access to a spring compressor I couldn't replace the lower ball joints or bushings on my '88 formula, so I took it into the shop. With the parts I provided labor came to $385, so, already grumbling about having to spend my new computer money on labor, I left my car in good hands. Unfortunatly, I got a call a little while later, turns out the shocks were rust welded into the fram points  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> luckily, they could get me the Kybs offered at the fiero store for $65 so I saved a bit of cash there. Bad side is that I'm out just over $500  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> good part is I'll have my front end almost completely rebuilt, the last parts (upper ball joints and tie rods) all appear to be in good shape.||||reg||1
Z||000001||qwikgta||02-07-2001||07:51 PM||||Thanks, Its nice to know that there is some good places to take your ride when you need the work, Im still looking here for one.<P>Rob<P>------------------<BR>QWIKGTA<BR>88 T-Top coupe<BR>http://robsfieroproject.homestead.com/index.html||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfiero||02-07-2001||08:06 PM||||Just an FYI (I know it's too late).....you don't need a spring compressor to remove the front springs.  Just a jack and some jackstands.  <P>Also, you should always expect to replace the shocks anytime you remove them (unless they were recently replaced).  The top nut always rusts itself to the shock......I use a "nutbreaker" to remove these nuts, but the shock is usually rendered useless.<P><BR>Sorry you spent more than needed, but at least you got it fixed.||||reg||1
